{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Statistical Power.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Statistical Power.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"statistical power statistical power","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Statistical Power.md","fileName":"Statistical Power.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Statistical Power","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-06 18:44\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Statistical Power and Effect Size]] · [[Statistical Inference and Resampling]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Statistical Power and Effect Size","Statistical Inference and Resampling","Effect Size","analyzinghealthdatainrforsasusers.pdf","dataanalysisforthelifescienceswithr.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Statistical Power.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Statistical Power.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"statistical power statistical power","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Statistical Power.md","fileName":"Statistical Power.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Statistical Power","heading":"Statistical Power","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Statistical power is the probability that a study will reject a false null hypothesis under a specified effect, sample size, significance level, and analysis design. Higher power reduces the chance of missing an effect of the assumed magnitude.\n\nPower is conditional rather than an intrinsic property of a dataset. Changing the test, outcome prevalence, allocation ratio, or anticipated [[Effect Size]] changes the sample size required to achieve a target such as 80 percent.\n\nThe source expresses power as the probability that a test statistic exceeds the chosen cutoff when the alternative is true. Its simulations show that increasing sample size narrows standard errors, separates the alternative distribution from the null, and increases the chance of detection.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Statistical Power and Effect Size","Statistical Inference and Resampling","Effect Size","analyzinghealthdatainrforsasusers.pdf","dataanalysisforthelifescienceswithr.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Statistical Power.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Statistical Power.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"statistical power statistical power","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Statistical Power.md","fileName":"Statistical Power.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Statistical Power","heading":"References","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"[[analyzinghealthdatainrforsasusers.pdf]]\n\n[[dataanalysisforthelifescienceswithr.pdf]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Statistical Power and Effect Size","Statistical Inference and Resampling","Effect Size","analyzinghealthdatainrforsasusers.pdf","dataanalysisforthelifescienceswithr.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""}]}